1
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,866
I introduced this concept of levels of

2
00:00:04,866 --> 00:00:07,227
abstraction in the last lecture because

3
00:00:07,403 --> 00:00:09,877
levels of abstraction allow us to decide

4
00:00:10,214 --> 00:00:12,559
whether a function does one thing or if it

5
00:00:12,559 --> 00:00:13,651
does multiple things.

6
00:00:14,888 --> 00:00:17,489
Because functions should only do work that's

7
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:20,669
one level of abstraction below the level of

8
00:00:20,669 --> 00:00:23,239
abstraction implied by their function name.

9
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:26,932
Okay, that's a complex sentence. What does

10
00:00:26,948 --> 00:00:27,189
it mean?

11
00:00:28,119 --> 00:00:29,306
Consider this example.

12
00:00:29,916 --> 00:00:31,761
If you have a function which is called

13
00:00:31,825 --> 00:00:34,776
emailIsValid and it takes an email as a

14
00:00:34,776 --> 00:00:35,322
parameter,

15
00:00:36,076 --> 00:00:38,049
then you might have some low-level code in

16
00:00:38,081 --> 00:00:39,990
there as you can tell in this example.

17
00:00:40,567 --> 00:00:43,294
But this operation is one level below the

18
00:00:43,294 --> 00:00:44,850
function name implied level.

19
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:47,529
emailIsValid, if we would call this

20
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:49,823
function, is pretty high level, but it's

21
00:00:49,871 --> 00:00:52,100
clear that as a result, we want to know

22
00:00:52,261 --> 00:00:54,988
whether an email is valid or not. So inside

23
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,716
of that emailIsValid function, we perform an

24
00:00:57,716 --> 00:00:59,978
operation on a lower level of abstraction.

25
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:01,760
In this case, email includes@.

26
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,584
But writing this low-level code here is no

27
00:01:04,696 --> 00:01:07,087
problem because understanding it is quite

28
00:01:07,183 --> 00:01:09,349
easy because of the function name of the

29
00:01:09,349 --> 00:01:11,516
function in which we write it. The function

30
00:01:11,516 --> 00:01:13,923
is named emailIsValid, so it's clear that

31
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:15,768
we're doing this low-level work to get an

32
00:01:15,928 --> 00:01:16,955
answer to this question.

33
00:01:17,485 --> 00:01:20,758
So because the operation we do inside of the

34
00:01:20,758 --> 00:01:22,764
function is close to the level of

35
00:01:22,764 --> 00:01:25,123
abstraction implied by the function name,

36
00:01:25,492 --> 00:01:28,762
because it answers the question posed by the

37
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:30,013
function name, we could say.

38
00:01:30,542 --> 00:01:32,466
Because of that, it's easy to understand

39
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:34,437
even though it's low-level code.

40
00:01:35,111 --> 00:01:37,019
So low-level code is not bad, it's

41
00:01:37,035 --> 00:01:39,119
necessary. You need to write it somewhere,

42
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,988
but you should write it in places where

43
00:01:41,988 --> 00:01:45,050
interpretation is added by the name of the

44
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:47,824
function in which that low-level code runs.

45
00:01:48,962 --> 00:01:50,405
Now, on the other hand, if you have a look

46
00:01:50,405 --> 00:01:53,756
at this example here, then you see that this

47
00:01:53,852 --> 00:01:55,519
could be harder to interpret.

48
00:01:56,176 --> 00:01:59,286
This function is called saveUser. And having

49
00:01:59,318 --> 00:02:02,476
this low-level code in there is a bit more

50
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:05,394
difficult because here the function name

51
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:09,257
saveUser does not add any interpretation to

52
00:02:09,257 --> 00:02:11,966
the low-level code. We have to figure out as

53
00:02:11,998 --> 00:02:14,178
a developer that this low-level code is

54
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:17,144
probably executed to validate some input.

55
00:02:17,753 --> 00:02:19,677
It makes sense that we have a validation

56
00:02:19,677 --> 00:02:22,706
step as part of saving a user. But the

57
00:02:22,786 --> 00:02:25,191
actual low-level code is a bit harder to

58
00:02:25,207 --> 00:02:27,788
interpret than if it's close to the level of

59
00:02:27,788 --> 00:02:30,545
abstraction implied by the function name as

60
00:02:30,561 --> 00:02:31,956
we have it on the left side here.

61
00:02:32,982 --> 00:02:35,306
If a function is called saveUser, it should

62
00:02:35,306 --> 00:02:37,181
just orchestrate all the steps that are

63
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:39,554
required to save a user. So here we got a

64
00:02:39,554 --> 00:02:42,503
too big gap between the level of abstraction

65
00:02:42,503 --> 00:02:43,946
implied by the function name

66
00:02:44,507 --> 00:02:47,088
and the level of abstraction of the actual

67
00:02:47,088 --> 00:02:48,771
operation we do in this function.

68
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:51,951
And I know that this can sound super

69
00:02:52,048 --> 00:02:54,361
overwhelming and confusing. It is something

70
00:02:54,393 --> 00:02:56,979
that comes with experience and where just

71
00:02:57,188 --> 00:02:59,388
thinking about it is already worth a lot.

72
00:03:00,561 --> 00:03:02,745
Now, levels of abstraction also matter

73
00:03:02,745 --> 00:03:05,058
because of one other concept. And I will

74
00:03:05,139 --> 00:03:06,857
also come back to that example I showed you

75
00:03:06,857 --> 00:03:08,496
in the last lecture again, no worries.

76
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:11,467
But here's that other concept where levels

77
00:03:11,563 --> 00:03:12,945
of abstraction also matter.

78
00:03:13,507 --> 00:03:16,398
You should try not to mix multiple levels of

79
00:03:16,398 --> 00:03:18,743
abstraction in one and the same function.

80
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:20,109
Here's an example.

81
00:03:20,799 --> 00:03:23,626
In this code here, we're mixing different

82
00:03:23,626 --> 00:03:24,734
levels of abstraction.

83
00:03:25,313 --> 00:03:27,577
We got this quite low-level email includes

84
00:03:27,626 --> 00:03:30,340
code and the higher-level user save code.

85
00:03:31,272 --> 00:03:33,553
If we read this snippet, we need to add

86
00:03:33,649 --> 00:03:36,604
quite a lot of interpretation to it to fully

87
00:03:36,701 --> 00:03:37,889
understand what's going on.

88
00:03:38,644 --> 00:03:40,893
If this would be written like this, all the

89
00:03:40,893 --> 00:03:43,302
operations would be on the same level of

90
00:03:43,302 --> 00:03:45,568
abstraction and it would be easier to

91
00:03:45,616 --> 00:03:47,897
understand this. If you read that, you

92
00:03:48,026 --> 00:03:50,179
really just need to read the names of these

93
00:03:50,211 --> 00:03:52,750
functions, assuming that they are properly

94
00:03:52,782 --> 00:03:55,352
named, and you fully understand what this

95
00:03:55,352 --> 00:03:55,883
code does.

96
00:03:56,943 --> 00:03:59,594
This is why levels of abstraction matter and

97
00:03:59,674 --> 00:04:02,358
why mixing different levels or having a too

98
00:04:02,486 --> 00:04:04,912
big gap between a function name and the

99
00:04:04,912 --> 00:04:05,748
operations done,

100
00:04:06,278 --> 00:04:09,057
why that can cause problems and lead to bad

101
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:09,379
code.

102
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,301
And if we come back to the example from

103
00:04:12,301 --> 00:04:12,654
before,

104
00:04:13,265 --> 00:04:15,689
then the original function suffered from

105
00:04:15,753 --> 00:04:16,685
multiple problems.

106
00:04:17,343 --> 00:04:20,634
Most importantly, the gap between the level

107
00:04:20,634 --> 00:04:22,866
of abstraction implied by the function name,

108
00:04:23,444 --> 00:04:24,360
renderContent,

109
00:04:24,889 --> 00:04:27,314
and the levels of abstraction of the actual

110
00:04:27,314 --> 00:04:29,738
operations we're doing here was too big.

111
00:04:30,589 --> 00:04:32,725
We got a bunch of low-level operations in

112
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:35,053
there like comparing the element or

113
00:04:35,246 --> 00:04:37,783
concatenating our opening tags down there.

114
00:04:38,858 --> 00:04:40,991
In the clean function example, that's not

115
00:04:41,039 --> 00:04:41,408
the case.

116
00:04:42,082 --> 00:04:44,809
We got operations which are one level below

117
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:46,733
the level of abstraction implied by

118
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:47,616
renderContent here.

119
00:04:48,241 --> 00:04:50,487
Validate element type, create renderable

120
00:04:50,583 --> 00:04:53,551
content, render on root. It makes sense that

121
00:04:53,551 --> 00:04:55,941
these are the steps we need to go through to

122
00:04:56,101 --> 00:04:57,785
render some content to the screen.

123
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:00,913
So if we call renderContent, it makes sense

124
00:05:00,913 --> 00:05:02,357
that these are steps that need to be

125
00:05:02,437 --> 00:05:05,260
executed. The actual work which needs to be

126
00:05:05,324 --> 00:05:07,186
done, the lower-level operations

127
00:05:07,732 --> 00:05:09,852
are then outsourced into other functions.

128
00:05:10,462 --> 00:05:12,533
And in the functions to which we outsource

129
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:14,300
them, they then again make sense.

130
00:05:14,958 --> 00:05:17,848
In validateElementType, it makes sense that

131
00:05:17,848 --> 00:05:20,385
we do comparisons like this. Here, this

132
00:05:20,417 --> 00:05:22,697
low-level code is absolutely okay.

133
00:05:23,885 --> 00:05:25,973
And that's why levels of abstraction matter

134
00:05:26,053 --> 00:05:28,590
and how you can ensure that you write clean

135
00:05:28,702 --> 00:05:29,120
functions.

136
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:31,912
Now, as I mentioned before, This is a

137
00:05:31,912 --> 00:05:34,524
challenging and difficult concept. It is

138
00:05:34,637 --> 00:05:36,800
something which comes with experience and

139
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:38,851
even after years of development, you will

140
00:05:38,963 --> 00:05:40,838
still sometimes write functions which are

141
00:05:40,838 --> 00:05:41,495
not clean.

142
00:05:42,312 --> 00:05:45,277
As always, it's also not a hard rule. If you

143
00:05:45,277 --> 00:05:47,504
have a very short function with mixed levels

144
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:49,860
of abstraction, that could be fine if it's

145
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:50,629
easy to understand.

146
00:05:51,382 --> 00:05:54,283
Nonetheless, it is important to always think

147
00:05:54,283 --> 00:05:56,270
about that and to at least consider

148
00:05:56,270 --> 00:05:57,199
splitting a function.

149
00:05:58,116 --> 00:05:59,644
And we're going to have a demo and a

150
00:05:59,644 --> 00:06:02,058
challenge in the next lecture where we

151
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:04,617
practice this at a simpler example. And

152
00:06:04,633 --> 00:06:06,129
we're also going to learn about a couple of

153
00:06:06,306 --> 00:06:08,430
other related concepts throughout the rest

154
